    {
     "type": "grammar-note",
     "title": "Grammar: Ezafe chains",
     "body": "The **ezafe** is an unstressed `-e` (or `-ye` after a vowel) that links a noun to a following possessor or modifier — `کتابِ من` \"the book **of** me.\" The Persian Book of Mormon rarely writes it, so this guide restores it as a small superscript kasra ( ِ ). It chains down a noun phrase, each link binding the next word, and it drives almost every phrase in Nephi's editorial.",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ref": "Chapter summary",
       "ref_anchor": "chapter-summary",
       "persian": "امور{e} خدا",
       "translit": "omūr-e xodā",
       "en": "the things of God"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "به دست{e} پدرم",
       "translit": "be dast-e pedar-am",
       "en": "by the hand of my father"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 4",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-4",
       "persian": "کمال{e} نیّت{e} من",
       "translit": "kamāl-e niyyat-e man",
       "en": "the fulness of my intent"
      }
     ],
     "closing": "When the head already ends in `ۀ` (`خاندان-نامۀ`, `همۀ`) or in a long vowel spelled with `ی` (`سوی`, `خدای`), the ezafe is already written and this guide leaves it unmarked."
    },
    {
     "type": "grammar-note",
     "title": "Grammar: The object marker را",
     "body": "The particle `را` _rā_ marks a **definite direct object**, and it follows the *entire* object phrase — after any ezafe chain, plural, or relative clause — not the bare noun. Because Persian puts the verb last, `را` is the reader's cue that everything before it is the thing being acted on.",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "خاندان-نامۀ پدرانم را ... می آورم",
       "translit": "xāndān-nāme-ye pedarān-am rā ... mī-āvaram",
       "en": "I give the genealogy of my fathers"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 4",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-4",
       "persian": "آدمیان را ترغیب کنم",
       "translit": "ādamiyān rā tarġīb konam",
       "en": "that I may persuade men"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "grammar-note",
     "title": "Grammar: Possessive and pronominal suffixes",
     "body": "Persian attaches possession directly to the noun as an enclitic: `-am` (my), `-at` (your sg.), `-aš` (his/her), `-mān` (our), `-tān` (your pl.), `-šān` (their). After a plural in `-ān` the linking vowel drops (`پدران‌م`, `نوادگان‌م`). The very same clitic on a preposition or verb serves as an object pronoun (`مرا` = `من`+`را`).",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "پدرانم",
       "translit": "pedarān-am",
       "en": "my fathers"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "نگاشته ام",
       "translit": "negāšte-am",
       "en": "my record"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 6",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-6",
       "persian": "نوادگانم",
       "translit": "navādegān-am",
       "en": "my seed"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "grammar-note",
     "title": "Grammar: The passive voice with شدن",
     "body": "Persian has no inflected passive; it uses the **past participle** of the main verb plus a form of `شدن` \"to become\": `نوشته شد` \"was written\" (lit. \"became written\"). The participle is invariable, `شدن` carries tense and person, and the agent — if named — comes in with `به دست` \"by the hand of.\"",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "آورده شده",
       "translit": "āvarde šode",
       "en": "has been given"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "نگارش شده است",
       "translit": "negāreš šode ast",
       "en": "has been written"
      },
      {
       "ref": "Verse 3",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-3",
       "persian": "نوشته شوند",
       "translit": "nevešte šavand",
       "en": "be written"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "grammar-note",
     "title": "Grammar: The future tense (خواهد + short infinitive)",
     "body": "Formal Persian marks the definite future with the auxiliary `خواستن` \"to want\" — conjugated for person (`خواهم`, `خواهی`, `خواهد` …) — followed by the **short infinitive** (the bare past stem: `آورد`, `داد`). The auxiliary comes **first** and takes no `می-`; negation prefixes it (`نخواهم`).",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "خواهم آورد",
       "translit": "xāham āvord",
       "en": "shall I bring/give"
      }
     ],
     "closing": "Everyday speech usually lets the simple present carry future meaning; the `خواهد` future belongs to the formal, scriptural register of Nephi's declaration."
    },
    {
     "type": "grammar-note",
     "title": "Grammar: The correlative نه … نه (\"neither … nor\")",
     "body": "Repeated `نه` _ne_ \"not\" coordinates two negated options: \"neither X … nor Y.\" Nephi uses it to refuse the genealogy twice over — not here, and not at any later time. Each `نه` negates its own clause; the verbs themselves stay in their normal (here, future/present) form.",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ref": "Verse 1",
       "ref_anchor": "verse-1",
       "persian": "نه خاندان-نامۀ پدرانم را ... می آورم؛ نه ... خواهم آورد",
       "translit": "ne xāndān-nāme-ye pedarān-am rā ... mī-āvaram; ne ... xāham āvord",
       "en": "I neither give the genealogy … nor shall I give it"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
